@chapter Objective-C Frontend Language
@cindex Objective-C frontend
@cindex frontend, Objective-C

This chapter describes the Objective-C frontend language as implemented in the EightBol compiler.
The frontend consists of a lexer and parser that tokenize and parse Objective-C source code
into an intermediate representation suitable for code generation.

@menu
* Lexer::
* Parser::
@end menu


@node Lexer
@section Lexer

The Objective-C lexer transforms input source code into a stream of tokens.
It recognizes keywords, operators, literals, and identifiers according to the Objective-C
subset supported by EightBol.

@menu
* Keywords::
* Operators::
* Literals::
* Identifiers::
@end menu


@subsection Keywords
The lexer recognizes the following Objective-C keywords, which are mapped to token symbols:

@table @asis
@item @code{@@interface} @:: @code{:AT-INTERFACE}
@item @code{@@implementation} @:: @code{:AT-IMPLEMENTATION}
@item @code{@@end} @:: @code{:AT-END}
@item @code{@@property} @:: @code{:AT-PROPERTY}
@item @code{@@selector} @:: @code{:AT-SELECTOR}
@item @code{@@protocol} @:: @code{:AT-PROTOCOL}
@item @code{@public} @:: @code{:AT-PUBLIC}
@item @code{@private} @:: @code{:AT-PRIVATE}
@item @code{@protected} @:: @code{:AT-PROTECTED}
@item @code{@synthesize} @:: @code{:AT-SYNTHESIZE}
@item @code{@autoreleasepool} @:: @code{:AT-AUTORELEASEPOOL}
@item @code{@try} @:: @code{:AT-TRY}
@item @code{@catch} @:: @code{:AT-CATCH}
@item @code{@finally} @:: @code{:AT-FINALLY}
@item @code{@throw} @:: @code:AT-THROW}
@item @code{@return} @:: @code{:AT-RETURN}
@item @code{if} @:: @code{:IF}
@item @code{else} @:: @code{:ELSE}
@item @code{for} @:: @code{:FOR}
@item @code{while} @:: @code{:WHILE}
@item @code{do} @:: @code{:DO}
@item @code{return} @:: @code{:RETURN}
@item @code{break} @:: @code{:BREAK}
@item @code{continue} @:: @code{:CONTINUE}
@item @code{switch} @:: @code{:SWITCH}
@item @code{case} @:: @code{:CASE}
@item @code{default} @:: @code{:DEFAULT}
@item @code{self} @:: @code{:SELF}
@item @code{super} @:: @code{:SUPER}
@item @code{nil} @:: @code{:NIL}
@item @code{yes} @:: @code{:YES}
@item @code{no} @:: @code{:NO}
@item @code{true} @:: @code{:TRUE}
@item @code{false} @:: @code{:FALSE}
@item @code{id} @:: @code{:ID-TYPE}
@item @code{void} @:: @code{:VOID}
@item @code{class} @:: @code{:CLASS}
@item @code{protocol} @:: @code{:PROTOCOL}
@end table

These keywords are case-insensitive in the lexer (matched via @code{string-equal}).
The @code{@} prefixed keywords are recognized only when the lexeme begins with @code{@}.
Analogous to reader macros in Lisp (e.g., @code{#x} for hexadecimal), the @code{@} prefix
acts as a directive indicator that triggers special token handling.

@subsection Operators
The lexer recognizes the following operators, each mapped to a token symbol:

@table @asis
@item @code{+} @:: @code{:PLUS}
@item @code{-} @:: @code{:MINUS}
@item @code{*} @:: @code{:TIMES}
@item @code{/} @:: @code{:DIVIDE}
@item @code{%} @:: @code{:MODULO}
@item @code{=} @:: @code{:EQUAL}
@item @code{==} @:: @code{:EQUAL-EQUAL}
@item @code{!=} @:: @code{:NOT-EQUAL} (also @code{<>})
@item @code{<} @:: @code{:LT}
@item @code{>} @:: @code{:GT}
@item @code{<=} @:: @code{:LE}
@item @code{>=} @:: @code{:GE}
@item @code{&&} @:: @code{:AND-AND}
@item @code{||} @:: @code{:OR-OR}
@item @code{!} @:: @code{:NOT}
@item @code{[} @:: @code{:LBRACKET}
@item @code{]} @:: @code{:RBRACKET}
@item @code{.} @:: @code{:DOT}
@item @code{:} @:: @code{:COLON}
@item @code{;} @:: @code{:SEMICOLON}
@item @code{(} @:: @code{:LPAREN}
@item @code{)} @:: @code{:RPAREN}
@item @code{\{} @:: @code{:LBRACE}
@item @code{\}} @:: @code{:RBRACE}
@item @code{,} @:: @code{:COMMA}
@item @code{->} @:: @code{:ARROW}
@item @code{+=} @:: @code{:PLUS-EQUAL}
@item @code{-=} @:: @code{:MINUS-EQUAL}
@item @code{*=} @:: @code{:TIMES-EQUAL}
@item @code{/=} @:: @code{:DIVIDE-EQUAL}
@item @code{%=} @:: @code{:MODULO-EQUAL}
@end table

Operators are matched by exact string comparison. Compound operators (e.g., @code{+=}) are
recognized as single tokens. The lexer does not support reader macros for operators, but
the tokenization of operators parallels how Lisp reader macros like @code{#'} (sharp-quote)
transform input syntax.

@subsection Literals
The lexer recognizes three types of literals: numbers, strings, and identifiers.

@subsubsection Numbers
Numeric literals support multiple formats with prefixes, similar to Lisp reader macros like @code{#x} and @code{#o}.
The lexer scans numeric prefixes to determine the radix (base) of the number, then validates digits accordingly.

@table @asis
@item @code{Decimal} @:: No prefix required. Examples: @code{42}, @code{100}, @code{3.14}
@item @code{Hexadecimal} @:: Prefix @code{0x}. Examples: @code{0xFF}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}, @code{0x10}
@item @code{Octal} @:: Prefix @code{0o}. Examples: @code{0o755}, @code{0o644}, @code{0o77}
@item @code{Binary} @:: Prefix @code{0b}. Examples: @code{0b1010}, @code{0b11110000}, @code{0b101}
@item @code{Dword (Extended Decimal)} @:: Prefix @code{0d}. Examples: @code{0d42}, @code{0d1000}. Supports floating-point notation.
@end table

All numeric formats produce a @code{:NUMBER} token. For example:
@itemize
@item @code{42} @→ @code{(:NUMBER "42")}
@item @code{0xFF} @→ @code{(:NUMBER "0xFF")}
@item @code{0o755} @→ @code{(:NUMBER "0o755")}
@item @code{0b1010} @→ @code{(:NUMBER "0b1010")}
@end itemize

The lexer validates that hexadecimal literals contain only 0-9 and A-F (case-insensitive),
octal literals contain only 0-7, and binary literals contain only 0-1. Invalid digits for a given
format result in a scan error.

@subsubsection Strings
String literals are enclosed in double quotes (@code{"..."}). The lexer scans from the
opening quote to the closing quote, handling escaped quotes (@code{\"}) within the string.
The token value excludes the surrounding quotes. For example, @code{"hello"} produces
(@code{:STRING} . @code{"hello"}).

@subsection Identifiers
Identifiers consist of alphanumeric characters and underscores, and must not begin with a
digit. The lexer recognizes identifiers that are not keywords as @code{:IDENT} tokens.

@subsubsection Identifier Normalization
The frontend automatically normalizes all identifiers to @strong{PascalCase} during lexing,
consistent with the Eightbol naming convention (Header-Case). This ensures uniform identifier
style in the generated AST regardless of source convention.

Normalization rules:
@itemize
@item The first character is converted to uppercase.
@item Subsequent characters are converted to lowercase.
@item Leading and trailing underscores are trimmed.
@end itemize

Examples:
@itemize
@item @code{myVariable} @→ @code{Myvariable}
@item @code{MY_CONST} @→ @code{My_const}
@item @code{_private} @→ @code{Private}
@item @code{className} @→ @code{Classname}
@end itemize

Objective-C conventionally uses @code{camelCase} for variables and @code{UpperCamelCase}
for class names in source code, but the frontend converts all identifiers to PascalCase
in AST nodes for consistency with the Eightbol code generation pipeline.


@node Parser
@section Parser

The Objective-C parser transforms a token stream into an EightBol abstract syntax tree (AST).
It implements a subset of Objective-C suitable for 8-bit/16-bit targets, mapping constructs
to EightBol primitives like @code{:set}, @code{:call}, @code{:method}, and control structures.

@menu
* Grammar Overview::
* Program Structure::
* Data Definitions::
* Statements::
* Expressions::
* Control Structures::
@end menu


@subsection Grammar Overview
The parser is defined using YACC-like syntax in Common Lisp. The start symbol is
@code{objective-program}. The parser recognizes terminals for punctuation, operators,
keywords, and literals (numbers, strings, identifiers).

Precedence levels (from lowest to highest):
@enumerate
@item @code{or} (@code{||})
@item @code{and} (@code{&&})
@item @code{not} (@code{!})
@item Equality and inequality (@code{=!=}, @code{<}, @code{<=}, @code{>}, @code{>=})
@item Addition and subtraction (@code{+}, @code{-})
@item Multiplication and division (@code{*}, @code{/})
@item Assignment (@code{=}) (right-associative)
@end enumerate


@subsection Program Structure
An @code{objective-program} consists of optional comments followed by an
@code{objective-chunk}. The chunk combines data definitions and statements.

@example
(objective-program
  (comments* objective-chunk
    (lambda (_c chunk _end)
      (declare (ignore _c _end))
      chunk)))
@end example


@subsection Data Definitions
Data definitions declare variables and arrays. They are parsed into @code{:dd} (data
definition) AST nodes.

@subsubsection Variable Declaration
@code{obj-var-decl> parses a variable declaration of the form:
@example
keyword-local ident = number
@end example
where @code{keyword-local} is a locally scoped variable keyword (e.g., @code{local} in
EightBol), @code{ident} is the variable name, and @code{number} is the initial value.
Produces:
@example
(list :dd :level 01 :label <var> :attr (list :usage :binary :value <val>))
@end example

@subsubsection Array Declaration
@code{obj-array-decl> parses an array declaration of the form:
@example
keyword-local ident = left-brace number-list right-brace
@end example
where @code{number-list} is a comma-separated list of numbers.
Produces:
@example
(list :dd :level 01 :label <arr> :attr (list :usage :binary :occurs (list 'length <elements>)))
@end example


@subsection Statements
The parser recognizes six types of statements, each mapped to an EightBol AST node.

@subsubsection If Statement
@code{if-statement> parses:
@example
keyword-if condition keyword-then block keyword-else else-block keyword-end
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-if}):
@example
(list :if :condition <cond> :then <blk> :else <else-block>)
@end example
Note: The @code{else} clause is optional in the grammar but required in this production.

@subsubsection While Statement
@code{while-statement> parses:
@example
keyword-while condition keyword-do block keyword-end
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-while}):
@example
(list :while :cond <cond> :body <blk>)
@end example
Maps to EightBol @code{:perform :until} construct.

@subsubsection For Statement
@code{for-statement> parses:
@example
keyword-for ident = number keyword-to number keyword-do block keyword-end
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-for}):
@example
(list :for :var <var> :start <start> :end <end> :body <blk>)
@end example
Maps to EightBol @code{:perform :varying} construct.

@subsubsection Call Statement
@code{call-statement> parses:
@example
ident left-paren args right-paren
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-call}):
@example
(list :call :target <fn> :args <args>)
@end example

@subsubsection Method Statement
@code{method-statement> parses:
@example
ident colon ident left-paren args right-paren
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-method}):
@example
(list :method :object <obj> :target <method> :args <args>)
@end example
Represents Objective-C message sending (e.g., @code{[obj method:arg]}).

@subsubsection Set Statement
@code{set-statement> parses:
@example
ident = expr
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-set}):
@example
(list :set :target <lhs> :value <rhs>)
@end example

@subsubsection Return Statement
@code{return-statement> parses:
@example
keyword-return expr?
@end example
Produces (via @code{parse/obj-return}):
@example
(list :exit-method :value <expr>)
@end example
Note: The expression is optional; if absent, @code{:value} is @code{nil}.


@subsection Expressions
Expressions are used in statements and include literals, identifiers, and binary operations.

The @code{expr> nonterminal produces:
@itemize
@item @code{number} @-> @code{:NUMBER} token value
@item @code{ident} @-> @code{:IDENT} token value
@item @code{string} @-> @code{:STRING} token value
@item Binary operations: @code{expr :op expr} for @code{:+}, @code{:-}, @code{:*}, @code{:/},
  @code{:eq} (@code{==}), @code{:neq} (@code{!=}), @code{:lt} (@code{<}), @code{:le} (@code{<=}),
  @code{:gt} (@code{>}), @code{:ge} (@code{>=})
@end itemize

For example, @code{x + 5 * y> parses as:
@example
(expr :plus (ident "x") (expr :times (number "5") (ident "y")))
@end example


@subsection Control Structures
Control structures are implemented as statements that alter flow of execution.

@subsubsection Conditional (@code{if})
The @code{if> statement evaluates a condition and executes one of two blocks.
Condition evaluation follows standard truthiness: @code{nil} or @code{false} is false;
all other values are true.

@subsubsection Loops (@code{while}, @code{for})
The @code{while> loop repeats a block while a condition is true.
The @code{for> loop iterates a variable from a start value to an end value (inclusive).
Both map to EightBol @code{:perform> constructs with @code{:until> and @code{:varying> respectively.

@subsubsection Jump Statements (@code{break}, @code{continue})
Note: The current grammar does not explicitly include @code{break> or @code{continue> statements.
These would need to be added to the @code{objective-statement> rule to be supported.
Analogous to how Lisp reader macros like @code{#.> (read-time evaluation) affect parsing,
adding such statements would require extending the grammar.


@node Analogies to Reader Macros
@section Analogies to Reader Macros

In Lisp, reader macros (e.g., @code{#x} for hexadecimal, @code{#' > for function
notation) extend the reader's tokenization syntax. Similarly, the Objective-C frontend
uses specific patterns to trigger special handling:

@itemize
@item The @code{@> prefix (as in @code{@interface>) acts like a reader macro keyword,
  signaling Objective-C directives.
@item Compound operators (e.g., @code{+=>) are single tokens, analogous to how
  @code{#> + > might be a reader macro for increment-if-bound.
@item String delimiters (@code{"..."}) function like the @code{#\" > reader macro
  for canonical strings in some Lisp implementations.
@end itemize

These analogies help readers familiar with Lisp understand how the frontend
recognizes and processes special syntax patterns.


@node Unsupported Features
@section Unsupported Features

The Objective-C frontend intentionally omits certain features to match EightBol's
8-bit/16-bit target constraints:

@itemize
@item Preprocessor directives (beyond @code{@> keywords)
@item Exception handling (@code{@try>, @code{@catch>, @code{@finally>, @code{@throw>)
@item Automatic Reference Counting (ARC)
@item Blocks and Grand Central Dispatch
@item Non-floating-point numeric types (e.g., @code{float>, @code{double>)
@item Variadic methods
@item Properties with custom getters/setters (beyond @code{@synthesize>)
@item Dynamic method resolution (@code{resolveInstanceMethod:>)
@end itemize

These exclusions ensure the generated code remains efficient for resource-constrained
systems. Features like exception handling are replaced with fault logging (see
@code{:log-fault> nodes in the AST).

@node Conclusion
@section Conclusion

The Objective-C frontend provides a practical subset of Objective-C for retro game
development. By mapping Objective-C constructs to EightBol primitives, it enables
object-oriented programming while respecting the limitations of 8-bit/16-bit CPUs.
The lexer and parser are designed for simplicity and efficiency, with clear
analogies to Lisp reader macros for those familiar with that ecosystem.
